{"entity": "openEuler", "type": "organization", "description": "openEuler is an open-source, Linux-based operating system project initiated by Huawei Technologies Co., Ltd. and now incubated and operated by the OpenAtom Foundation, officially launched on December 31, 2019. Designed as a stable, secure, and high-performance platform, openEuler serves as a foundational operating system for enterprise computing, cloud environments, edge computing, server infrastructure, and embedded systems. It supports multiple processor architectures, including x86_64, aarch64 (ARM64), RISC-V, Kunpeng, Phytium, and Zen, enabling broad hardware compatibility and reflecting its commitment to an inclusive, architecture-agnostic ecosystem. The project is built on the Linux kernel, with specific versions such as 4.19.90 used in certain iterations, and fosters a community-driven development model with transparent governance, Special Interest Groups (SIGs), committer and maintainer roles, and public release mechanisms.\n\nopenEuler provides long-term support (LTS) releases, including openEuler 20.03-LTS (SP1–SP4), 22.03-LTS (SP1, SP2, SP3), and 24.03-LTS (SP1, SP2, SP3), which are widely deployed in cloud-native platforms, virtual machines, data centers, and enterprise-grade applications. These versions are tailored for stability, security, and reliability, making them suitable for container environments, system-level testing, and large-scale deployments. The platform is promoted as a migration target for legacy systems running CentOS, Red Hat Enterprise Linux (RHEL), and similar distributions, supported by tools like x2openEuler for streamlined upgrades. It features robust software package management via YUM and DNF, with signed RPM packages verified using the RPM-GPG-KEY-openEuler, and repositories hosted on official mirrors like repo.openeuler.openatom.cn, as well as mirrors from Huawei Cloud and Tsinghua University.\n\nThe system includes advanced functionality such as minimal installation modes, security enhancements—including PAM_wheel.so enforcement—default GRUB credentials (root/openEuler#12), and hardware compatibility testing via oec-hardware. It integrates system-level software such as SysMaster, the iSulad container runtime, and Euler-Copilot, and supports critical operations like IPSec/VPN configuration, driver installation, and software compilation. openEuler is utilized in diverse domains, including AI development, real-time operating system integration (e.g., with UniProton), embedded devices, system diagnostics, and deployment of databases like MySQL and OpenGauss. It supports software components such as Zabbix, Atlas 200 drivers, ROS2 Humble, and hardware platforms like LeapFive InFive Poros and VisionFive development boards.\n\nThe project plays a central role in China’s information technology innovation (xinchuang) initiative and is widely adopted across government, finance, transportation, energy, education, and communications sectors. It has achieved over 3 million installations and holds more than 25% market share in new Chinese server operating systems. OpenEuler continues to expand its global presence, documented on DistroWatch, and maintains active community engagement through technical forums, internships, documentation, and open contribution channels. While it faces certain challenges—including limited support for snapd, absence of EPEL, missing package compatibility with some CentOS tools, and known issues such as kernel bugs affecting UDP transmission—its ecosystem grows rapidly, with initiatives like open-eBackup, secGear for confidential computing, the Gazelle networking stack, and the COPR repository. It is also the foundation for tools such as EulerMaker and Eggops for Kubernetes deployment. Despite minor installation limitations and dependency management complexities, openEuler stands as a robust, adaptable, and innovative open-source platform, symbolizing a major advancement in China's open-source software landscape.<SEP>openEuler is a Linux-based open-source operating system designed for enterprise use.<SEP>openEuler is a Linux-based open-source operating system designed for enterprise use.<SEP>openEuler is an open-source operating system project under the Linux Foundation, targeted by the HCIA certification.<SEP>openEuler is an open-source operating system project focused on server, cloud, and edge computing, released as part of the OpenEuler community."}
